.\" No version in the title line: see the note in irontile.1.
.TH IRONTILECTL 1 "" "irontile" "irontile"
.SH NAME
irontilectl \- control a running irontile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B irontilectl
.I ACTION...
.br
.B irontilectl
.I SUBCOMMAND
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every binding is also a command.
.B irontilectl
connects to a running compositor's control socket and asks it either to do
something \(em anything the configuration file accepts as a binding \(em or to
say what it currently has.
.PP
Words are joined, so both
.B irontilectl focus left
and
.B "irontilectl \(dqfocus left\(dq"
work.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B frame
Where every window is right now.
.TP
.B outputs
Connected displays and their arrangement.
.TP
.B workspaces
Every desktop, and what is on it.
.TP
.B windows
Every window, with its title and application id.
.TP
.B layers
Panels and overlays on screen, and what they reserve.
.TP
.B layout
The whole layout engine state, as JSON.
.TP
.B watch
Stream events until interrupted.
.TP
.B version
Show the version and exit. This asks the compositor that is running, which is
not always the one installed.
.TP
.B help
Show a usage message, including every action, and exit.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.BI focus " direction"
Move the keyboard focus. A direction is
.BR left ", " right ", " up ", " down ,
or any of their other spellings.
.TP
.BI move " direction"
Move the focused window within the tree.
.TP
.BI resize " direction"
Grow or shrink the focused window.
.TP
.BI output " direction"
Focus the display in that direction.
.TP
.BI send\-to\-output " direction"
Send the focused desktop to the display in that direction.
.TP
.B split horizontal|vertical|toggle
How the next window splits the container the focus is in.
.TP
.B equalize
Give every sibling in the container the same share.
.TP
.B float
Take the focused window out of the tree, or put it back.
.TP
.B fullscreen
Fill the display with the focused window, or stop.
.TP
.B close
Ask the focused window to close.
.TP
.BI workspace " n"
Show that desktop.
.B workspace next
and
.B workspace prev
step between them. Desktops are numbered without limit, so stepping past the
last one makes the next rather than stopping.
.TP
.BI move\-to\-workspace " n"
Send the focused window to that desktop.
.TP
.BI spawn " program " [ args... ]
Run a program. Everything after the program name is passed to it.
.TP
.BI vt " n"
Switch to that virtual terminal. This one fires even while the screen is
locked, because a machine with no way out of a wedged compositor is a machine
that has to be held down.
.TP
.BI warp " x y"
Put the pointer at that point in the space the displays share.
.TP
.B click|press|release [1|2|3]
Synthesize a pointer button where the pointer is: 1 is left, 2 middle, 3 right.
It does everything a real one does \(em focuses the
window under it, starts a drag, reaches the client.
.TP
.B reload
Read the configuration file again.
.TP
.B quit
End the session. This one never fires while the screen is locked.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IRONTILE_SOCKET
The socket to connect to, for targeting a specific instance. Otherwise the one
belonging to
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/irontile/<display>.sock
Where the compositor listens, keyed by Wayland display name so that two
compositors on one machine do not collide \(em which is exactly what happens
when a nested instance is run for development from inside a session.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
irontilectl focus left
irontilectl workspace 3
irontilectl send\-to\-output right
irontilectl spawn kitty
irontilectl watch
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irontile (1)
